<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-reals" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Real Numbers</title>

    <question>
      <p>What is a "number"?</p>
    </question>



    <observation>
      <p>Things we think of as "numbers" seem to have the following properties:</p>
          <aside>
            <p>In particular, these operations should both be commutative and associative, and obey distributive rules.</p>
          </aside>
        <ol>
          <li>There are operations <m>+</m> and <m>\times</m> that behave as we expect.</li>
          <li>There are identity elements: <m>0</m> for addition and <m>1</m> for multiplication.</li>
          <li>These operations have inverse operations <m>-</m> and <m>\div</m>, respectively.</li>
          <li>Every number has an additive inverse and every non-zero number has a multiplicative inverse.</li>
        </ol>
      <p>Mathematicians call anything with these properties a <term>field</term>.</p>
    </observation>

    <example>
      <p>The set of rational numbers, denoted <m>\Q</m>, is a field.</p>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>The set of <term>constructible numbers</term> are the lengths that can be
      constructed with a compass and straight edge, given a line segment of length <m>1</m>.
      The number <m>\sqrt{2}</m> is constructible, but <m>\sqrt[3]{2}</m> is not.
      </p>
      <aside>
        <p>A classical geometry question asks if, given a cube, another cube of twice
        the volume can be constructed with a compass and a straight edge.  Showing
        that this is impossible is equivalent to showing that <m>\sqrt[3]{2}</m> is
        not constructible and took over 2000 years to solve.
        </p>
      </aside>
      <p>
      The set of constructible numbers also forms a field.
      </p>
    </example>

    <example>
      <p>The set of rational functions, i.e. functions of the form <m>\dfrac{p(x)}{q(x)}</m>,
        where <m>p(x)</m> and <m>q(x)</m> are polynomials, is a field.
      </p>
    </example>

    <observation>
      <p>The rational numbers (and the constructible numbers) are an <term>ordered field</term>: we have an inequality operator <m>\lt</m> such that
        for any two distinct rational numbers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, either <m>a\lt b</m> or <m>b \lt a</m>.
        </p>
    </observation> 

  <activity xml:id="activity-q-lub">
    <introduction>
      <p>Consider the set of numbers <md>E=\{x \in \Q\ \mid\ x^2 \lt 2 \}</md>.</p>
      <aside>
        <p>This an example of what we call <term>set builder notation</term>.  The curly braces <m>\{</m> and <m>\}</m>
          denote a set, and <m>\in</m> means "is an element of" or "in", and <m>\mid</m> means "such that".  This whole
          set <m>E</m> is then "The set of all <m>x</m> that are elements of <m>\Q</m> such that <m>x^2 \lt 2</m>"
        </p>
        </aside>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <p>
        Find a rational number <m>a \in \Q</m> that is an <term>upper bound</term> for <m>E</m>; that is,
        <m>a \gt x</m> for every <m>x \in E</m>.
      </p>
    </task>
    <task>
      <p>
        What is the smallest rational number <m>a \in \Q</m> that is an upper bound for <m>E</m>?
      </p>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <definition>
    <p>An ordered set <m>S</m> has the <term>least-upper-bound property</term> if every nonempty subset
    <m>E \subset S</m> that has an upper bound has a least upper bound.
    </p>
    <p>An ordered field that has the least-upper-bound property is called <term>complete</term>.</p>
  </definition>

  <example>
    <p>The rational numbers <m>\Q</m> does not have the least upper bound property as we saw in <xref ref="activity-q-lub"/>.
    In other words, <m>\Q</m> is not complete.
    </p>
  </example>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-r-uniqe">
    <statement>
      <p>
        There is a unique complete ordered field that contains the rational numbers.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>Note that this theorem is saying two things: there exist complete ordered fields that contain the rationals,
    and further any two such fields are actually the same (i.e. it is unique).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition xml:id="def-reals">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The real numbers, denoted <m>\R</m>, are the unique complete ordered field containing <m>\Q</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      You can think about "building" <m>\R</m> from <m>\Q</m> by formally adding least upper bounds to every
      subset of <m>\Q</m>.  
    </p>
  </remark>
  

    

</section>